# AIRE — AI Integrated Real Estate

**HackUTD 2025 · React + TypeScript · Property discovery prototype**

AIRE helps users browse a small collection of Dallas-area real estate listings. Enter natural-language preferences (for example, "3 bed home under $400k") to get up to three matched listing cards. Open a card for its details and a link to the listing provider. Use **View more** to see additional results without duplicates.

> **Data disclosure:** The included `listings.json` is a historical, static sample dataset. It is **not** a live MLS feed; prices, availability, and listing URLs may be outdated. The demo runs without credentials using deterministic ranking. Optionally, configure `GEMINI_API_KEY` on the server to enable Gemini-backed ranking.

## Quick start

Requires Node.js 20 or newer and npm.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/SushantBotla/AIRE.git
cd AIRE
npm install
npm run dev
```

Open the local URL reported by Vite (normally http://127.0.0.1:5173). `npm run dev` starts Vite and a local API on port 3001. No API keys or database accounts are needed for local demo mode. To enable AI ranking, copy `.env.example` to `.env`, set `GEMINI_API_KEY`, and start the app. Never add `VITE_` to a private key variable.

```bash
npm test
npm run build
npm run preview
```

The API validates requests and responds at `POST /api/recommend` with JSON such as `{"query":"3 bed home under $400k","excludeIds":[]}`. On Vercel, the `api/` directory is a serverless function; the web UI is a Vite static build.

## Code tour

| Path | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `App.tsx` | React state, chat flow, recommendation requests, property browsing |
| `components/ChatMessage.tsx` | Chat messages and inline results |
| `components/PropertyCard.tsx` | Property preview cards |
| `components/PropertyDetailPage.tsx` | Property detail overlay and provider link |
| `api/recommend.ts` | Validated API, recommendation handling and duplicate filtering |
| `api/ai.ts` | Optional server-side Gemini ranking (when configured) |
| `dev-server.ts` | Local API endpoint for Vite development |
| `listings.json` | Bundled historical listing snapshot |
| `tests/recommend.test.ts` | Basic matching and exclusion tests |

## Design and tradeoffs

The initial hackathon build used the Gemini model to interpret preferences and a Supabase table for listing data. The bundled dataset supports local development without external accounts. Its results are not real-time or independent real-estate valuations; results are AI-selected only when a server-side Gemini key is configured. The original team created this under hackathon time constraints.

Future improvements include stronger schema validation, reliable data licensing and freshness checks, accessibility improvements, broader test coverage, and mobile layout refinements.

